items.prose:
Write the function a risk system calls before it uses a GARCH fit. Fit as in lesson 7, then refuse with `ValueError` when:

- the optimiser did not converge;
- $\hat\alpha + \hat\beta \ge 0.999$, too close to 1 for a long run;
- $\hat\alpha < 0.01$: the variance does not react, so $\hat\beta$ means nothing.

Otherwise return a dictionary of the six numbers named in the question.

items.prompt:
Your GARCH(1,1) fit on two years (504 days) of a share's daily returns, in %, gives $\hat\omega = 0.002$, $\hat\alpha = 0.07$, $\hat\beta = 0.929$. The risk report wants the share's long-run volatility.

Write a note to the head of risk (about 250 words). Cover:

(a) what $\hat\alpha + \hat\beta = 0.999$ means for how shocks fade, with the half-life;

(b) the long-run volatility the fit implies, and why you do not trust it;

(c) two reasons a fit can land this close to 1;

(d) what you would do instead, and what you would report.
